Z||000012||TheFiero64||05-02-2001||02:29 PM||||Believe it or not, Maaco. They did a great job. Not like I hear in stories about them. It was $812 and I did most all of the prep myself.<P>I know a lot of Maacos suck, but this one seems great. It is in Attleboro MA.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||05-02-2001||02:30 PM||||I think the best car alarm would be one that is silent at the car, but sets off a beeper that you carry around, on your belt, next to your 9mm.<P>  That way you can sneak up on them, and you will be face to face, and youll 'have to' shoot em.<P>   Its alot harder to explain why you shot someone in the back as they were running away.<P>  the hidden kill switch is the best way to keep your car from being driven away, esp if nowbody can see you flip it when you get out of the car (so they dont know its there).<P>  The cheapest way to go is to get a blinking led from radio shack, and a resistor, and stick it in your dash somewhere so it blinks whenever the ignition is off. Then at least the SOBs will think there is an alarm in the car, and they wont know what sets it off.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-02-2001||02:43 PM||||$800 bucks! Wow, the price has gone up. Last time I had them spay a car I prepped the bug myself, I think I even masked it if I remember right, and they did it for $200 (I think I might have bought the paint somewhere else too). But that was in '91.<P>  Do fieros need to be sprayed by a pro, is there something about the paint you need for the plastic panels that makes it necessary. I need to do my hood for certain, and maybe I will do the roof and fenders too.<P> I know its a hassel to get a space proper to spray a whole car, and then baking the paint on is another issue (is that still necessary with epoxy paints anymore?)<P>  But I was thinking, if you take the panels off  and spray them one at a time, you might be able to set up a decent booth with 1 mil plastic  big enough to do the panels and keep the dust off them while they dry.<P>  $800? Id have a hard time justifying that for my 85 SE, which Im trying to keep my overall expenditure to a min on. I think I would be inclined to get a compressor and spraygun and do it myself.<P>  But then, i dont expect my car to ever be anywheres near as nice as yours.<P>anyone spraying their own cars? I be interested in hearing how they turned out, and how much work it was.||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||05-02-2001||02:50 PM||||You spray em the same way as any other. And you dont have as far to reach to do center of hood, roof, or trunk lid. Theyre so low though, Id recommend putting it up on stands to get under front and rear facias.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fiero5||05-02-2001||03:05 PM||||That price is not bad. I can get mine done locally for around the same price though.<BR>But as long as they paint stays on yours, I would say you got a great deal!<P>Wow, V6, wing, deck scoop and now new paint. Your Fiero is looking sweeter everytime I see pics of her.<P>Nice job Josh.<P>See ya<P>Steve||||reg||12
Z||000017||Cheever2||05-03-2001||01:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I think the best car alarm would be one that is silent at the car, but sets off a beeper that you carry around<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><I>I think so, too.  Many years ago there was such a pager alarm in the JC Whitney catalog.  Don't know if they still have it.</I><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The cheapest way to go is to get a blinking led from radio shack, and a resistor, and stick it in your dash somewhere so it blinks whenever the ignition is off. Then at least the SOBs will think there is an alarm in the car, and they wont know what sets it off.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><I>I have a "Dashlight", it's a mini-flashlight that stays charged while plugged into the lighter socket.  It has a steady red light, and I think it may have the effect you're talking about.</I><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||pontiacs4me||05-03-2001||02:42 AM||||800 bucks, that seems like alot.  My dad had a schieb job done 2 years ago on his transam, its starting to chip off now, and it was only 350 or so.  I do paint work, and I can brag and say Im damn good at it too.  Check my post tomorrow that will be titled "Fiero is done, heres some pics."  If someone brings me a car all prepped out, Ill tape it and spray it for 250+ material cost.  So on average your looking at about 450-500 for a paint job.  And Ill sand and buff it out for an extra hundred.  And it will be damn sharp.  Guaranteed. Ive only painted about a dozen cars in my so far short life, but Im a natural.  Just find my post tomorrow and you can see for yourself.<BR>Oh yeah and if anyone wants their fiero painted, and youll be in the wichita area, send me an email.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Spektyr||05-03-2001||04:14 AM||||I haven't even seen pontiacs4me's car yet, but he's who I'll be having paint my car when I'm ready.  I've seen the attention to detail that went into the prep on his car, so I feel confident that my car will look better than new when he's done.<P>Of course I'm sure he'll charge me more than 250 + materials because for some strange reason I just HAVE to have the underside of the deck and hood and the inside of the doorframes all match.  I'll probably just have the body panels painted off the car.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Jaygee79||05-03-2001||09:23 AM||||They had the prices listed on the wall when we went in.  It was 2 something for just the basics (1 coat), but if you wanted the good stuff it was 5 something for just paint and and then another 2 something for the clearcoat.  Plus they did have to do a bit of sanding and stuff.  And it was out in less than a week.<P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooheey1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
